 #edb/ref 17592186083338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049976 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049976 #edb/ref 1031 "Linear Correlation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049976 #edb/ref 1033 734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049976 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "23404e79-b5c1-5a89-a64b-889c85ed49fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1031 "Making Predictions Using Trend Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1033 3753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1034 0.962541274292324 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "369e4b42-eb06-50fe-866b-3c2859ec00eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428733 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1031 "Trend Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1033 2609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1034 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "50060817-b302-5470-841b-1327afaae882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152046 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9683" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152046 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/79/793fd170c89b87cc168ead4bb92613135d503143a8b7f8dc7b7c39a3d2d065fa.png)\n\nWhich of the lines $p,q,r,$ or $s$ is the trend line of the scatter plot above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152046 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd9a05fb-f01c-5ec2-a984-9d73584065ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152046 #edb/ref 1074 "The plot shows that the data points are scattered along a curve, not a straight line.\n\n![](images/e2/e2f6a85400c916f198f9dd9fd8b04318d1d4c956c850f5e1e81968993b5d3d53.png)\n\nTherefore, there is no linear trend in the data." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152047 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9694" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152047 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/db/dbc57d2facbcf4bcf3b7617480384ea411d26be05d3fbbcf5edc6a14bc5ae216.png)\n\nDetermine the equation of the trend line shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152047 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "245065cc-c95c-5f93-bf7d-bbb6412a27e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152047 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $(x_1,y_1)=(-4,5)$ and $(x_2,y_2)=(5,-4).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-5}{5-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9}{9} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-1$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny = - x + b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(5,-4)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x+b \\\\ -4 & =-5+b \\\\ 1 & =b \\\\ b & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny = - x + 1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152048 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152048 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ad/ad0deee6d845c0241108567ce6b0c744604f1f6f50d9af0405523cd9501a96a0.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152048 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a299957-af22-544b-af37-695f45065a3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152048 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](images/72/722b5393babd1405c0330e9b4ed08c5030ddf6c69bd8b9e3f6c6d138f02c098c.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $(x_1,y_1)\\approx(4,4)$ and $(x_2,y_2)\\approx(6,7).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-4}{6-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=1.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny = 1.5 x + b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,4)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1.5x+b \\\\ 4 & =1.5(4)+b \\\\ 4 & =6+b \\\\ -2 & =b \\\\ b & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny = 1.5x - 2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152236 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152236 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152236 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152236 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152236 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152236 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152236 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152236 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152236 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Trendline of a Given Scatter Plot" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152236 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186152046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152236 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "327d9a77-b2dd-57b1-b959-570684714bf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152237 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152237 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152237 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152237 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152237 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152237 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152237 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152237 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152237 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152237 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Equation of a Trendline" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152237 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186152047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152237 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "63426ad7-4275-5ac3-854d-e91ec00dcbe3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152238 #edb/ref 1039 "Estimating the Equation of a Trendline" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152238 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186152048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152238 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "890526df-fd1a-5340-8732-efc0c1a1b0ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152322 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152322 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152322 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152322 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152322 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152322 #edb/ref 1045 "Trend Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152322 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186153192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152322 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152322 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186152322 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "678f7812-7ca7-5434-8d96-4a946abcde57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152413 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152413 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133633" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152413 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152413 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c1/c1b4ec98039bb5c09f168afa77f8211585403b333ae70bce383dbf2101212848.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between age and years of work experience among a particular group of workers. Which of the following could be the trend line for this scatter plot?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152413 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa90d9a6-e51c-5604-b79f-b37b09fca968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152413 #edb/ref 1074 "The plot shows that the data points are scattered along a straight line.\n\n![](images/c8/c8597282ec934e535a01fa6f22be95b958d0393a5d0c5cd6358a9a7e620ba6a5.png)\n\nWe see that the line $q$ best fits the given data set." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152416 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152416 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152416 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152416 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/99/99cd0af2bcfd5148a49179203b6fff4831133d875fda9fa474d928eab5eb8f1c.png)\n\nWhat is the slope of the trend line shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152416 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3eefe541-d677-50bc-bb17-f1cdd88a5e62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152416 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,1)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(8,9).$\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-1}{8-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152418 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152418 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152418 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152418 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e4/e4b948bc417deab7c83cb40f53ad4b55fbfb169fc4ec761a9365856a9457a8f5.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152418 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "be8af3f3-2d6e-5282-b124-c4fc21f05bfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152418 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](images/fd/fd3ce3de75fa9296c721e3f3a28bef241eae6350819c4cc7a54707921c0c1fe4.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(2,7)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(8,4).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-7}{8-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{6} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,7):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+b \\\\ 7 & =-0.5(2)+b \\\\ 7 & =-1+b \\\\ 8 & =b \\\\ b & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+8.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153192 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153192 #edb/ref 1061 36301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153192 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1ffbde92-cc7c-5728-b132-09515bbba591" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153192 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153193 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153193 #edb/ref 1061 36302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153193 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bcb01224-dfa0-5083-a8d7-0bd17096ef14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153193 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153194 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153194 #edb/ref 1061 36339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153194 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "07f5aa05-0a03-5c26-b1de-042a48f46f64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153194 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153195 #edb/ref 1061 36342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153195 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6d384c7-ae08-5f09-badd-504d232dec0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153195 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153422 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to determine if the relationship between two variables is *linear*. How would we do this?\nOne way is to draw a scatter plot and try to find a straight line that fits the data. \n\nConsider the scatter plot below that shows the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y.$ \n\n![](images/db/db07023aea39829e5567002225639ca9cc8cdcea01eb9681814eb27fe35f8eb1.png)\n\nNotice that the data points are scattered along a straight line, as shown below. This line is called the **trend line**, **regression line,** or **line of best fit.** We also say that the data has a **linear trend**.\n\n![](images/73/73a1609767711f53605a3ea8804be412596e7ff5a6295a92358ad75e3ac84631.png)\n\nOn the other hand, some data sets do *not* show a linear trend. For example, consider the following scatter plot that shows the correspondence between the variables $a$ and $b.$ \n\n![](images/66/66f80436628f69486da4cf32904a53c43f54cfcb31bfd4cba501f3cd2ab200c7.png)\n\nFrom the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear trend." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153422 #edb/ref 1065 5946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153422 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3a179e75-41c8-5ad7-9406-3b20db2bbcfe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153422 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266231 #edb/ref 1080 "y=1.5x-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266231 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266231 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "099bdefa-f8d2-5acc-84b7-74bc261996fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266260 #edb/ref 1080 "y=-0.5x+4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266260 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266260 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0cab51bf-eaf2-5abc-b4a1-4acce4c0e8e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266308 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266308 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266308 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "12e1f18b-1852-5af1-8499-7eb19