5d326b26a8bb6831f38456b7b47878ef66a185090c7327cbb151c4e951be33.png)\n\nWhat is the slope of the trend line shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268948 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b2869212-3453-5179-ad24-03054774f50a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268948 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,2)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(5,1).$\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2}{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268954 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268954 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268954 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268954 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1c/1c9f30692222eaaed4cda6b608dcc1ccb01eefd6b5fa7730b5b8bb29c9f528b1.png)\n\nDetermine the equation of the trend line shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268954 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c42b7eb-86b2-59d5-8ce6-728291f9e718" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268954 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,2)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(7,8).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{7-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=\\frac{6}{5}$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{6}{5}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{6}{5}x+b \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{6}{5}(2)+b \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{12}{5}+b \\\\ 10 & =12+5b \\\\ -2 & =5b \\\\ b & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268955 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134025" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268955 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268955 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c8/c8f6d0161777412704a9a80c0336af04a6968454d421913055024b6d0cf11c71.png)\n\nEstimate the slope of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round your answer to the nearest $0.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268955 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fec0ce3a-bf15-5c41-9f72-214fd8c31558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268955 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](images/b5/b50fd1636cc2e1bb9d150eaba9cc25a3ccedff77e607bf22853004332a7c4ddc.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(3,6)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(5,3).$\n\nTherefore, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6}{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{2} \\\\  & =-1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268958 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268958 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134032" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268958 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268958 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d2/d23d6a35343aebcb986118bd7c5bc720f00480706057ec900e55c75bac296ebb.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268958 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "240005ae-034c-5e2f-9211-125231674030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268958 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](images/66/663e4d77d51816641a9fa861f37893d9f986d5e2b00e605ed5eb4fc19d40eab4.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(5,5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(7,9).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-5}{7-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=2$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=2x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(5,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x+b \\\\ 5 & =2(5)+b \\\\ 5 & =10+b \\\\ -5 & =b \\\\ b & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=2x-5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428733 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428733 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 2609->2585. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428733 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186048834 #edb/ref 17592186045436]}
